78	- **PT2.1** Keywords are **contextual**: a keyword is reserved only in the grammar positions that use it, and anywhere an identifier is expected it is an ordinary identifier. So a field may be named `state`, `derived`, `internal`, `terminal` or `type`. The parser decides with at most two tokens of lookahead: after a field's initial expression, `derived` or `internal` followed by `:` begins the next field, and otherwise it is the modifier. Field and action names lower verbatim into `contract.json`, traces and diagnostics, so a corpus's own names are kept. There is no second, external name for a field. Identifiers follow TypeScript identifier rules. Rejection reasons and effect kinds are string literals, so agents can reuse the names already present in a system.
79	- **PT2.2** The expression sublanguage (AD-2) is a total, side-effect-free subset of TypeScript expressions:
80	  - literals, field and parameter names, `pre.` and `post.` (in step properties), enum members;
81	  - **name resolution (S19).** A bare name resolves to the innermost binding: a local (`let` or a pattern binder), then a parameter, then a field, as in TypeScript. `pre.f` always names the pre-state field `f`, in handlers as in properties, so a field hidden by a parameter of the same name stays readable. Parameters are visible only where the action is known: in a handler, and in a guard, step property or reject property only where `action == A` narrows it (PT5). With no narrowing, no parameter is in scope and a bare name is the field;
82	  - `== != < <= > >= && || !`, `+ - *`, and integer `/` and `%`. Division truncates toward zero, and `%` takes the sign of the dividend, as in TypeScript with the quotient truncated (`Math.trunc(a / b)`). A divisor whose static range contains 0 is `PL1204 DIVISION_BY_ZERO`, so division is total;
83	  - `implies`;
84	  - `count(kind)` and `before(k1, k2)` (in path properties). Their arguments are effect kinds, string literals. `before("k1", "k2")` holds on a path iff every emission of `k2` is preceded by an emission of `k1` (S37);
85	  - `in { … }`;
86	  - in configuration properties only: `forall i in g: …`, `exists i in g: …`, the aggregates `count`, `sum`, `all` and `any` over `g[*]`, and `init(e)` for the initial value of `e` (PS13.6). The same quantifiers and aggregates range over a child family `f` in a compose invariant (S49);
87	  - map indexing `m[k]`, where `k` is a value of the map's key type;
88	  - map literals `{ k1: v1, …, kn: vn }`, which must list every key of the map's key type exactly once (`PL1502 INCOMPLETE_MAP_LITERAL`), and, for maps keyed by an integer range, the array literal `[v0, v1, …, vn]`; map update is the statement form `m' = m with [k] = v`, which writes one entry and leaves the others unchanged.
89	
90	  It has no loops, no calls to host functions and no string operations beyond equality and membership. Every expression terminates by construction. Precedence and associativity are fixed in PS12; the formatter parenthesises any mix of `implies` with `&&` or `||`.
91	  Primed fields are write-only: an expression never reads `f'` (PS2.3), and a field written twice on one path is `PL2102 DOUBLE_WRITE` (PS2.4).
92	- **PT2.3** Policies use PX, Polyaxion's three-valued (Kleene) language. The two logics are separated by **type**, not by syntax (PS8.0):
93	  - machine expressions have type `Bool`; policy inputs are optional, so policy expressions have type `Tri`;
94	  - one operator set (`==`, `!=`, `&&`, `||`, `!`, `in`) serves both, with its meaning given by the operand types;
95	  - `unknown(x)` has type `Bool` and is legal anywhere an optional value is in scope;
96	  - a `Tri` where a `Bool` is expected is `PL1203 TRI_WHERE_BOOL_EXPECTED`.
97	
98	  This follows the pattern of an option type, which agents know from TypeScript's `boolean | undefined`.
99	- **PT2.4** `e is C` tests a union value's constructor and has type `Bool`; `e is C1 | C2` is short for `e is C1 || e is C2`. `C { q: e }` constructs a union value. `match` is exhaustive (`PL1501 NON_EXHAUSTIVE_MATCH`). Enums are unions without payloads, so `==` and `is` agree on them.
100	  - **Names in patterns (S21).** In a payload pattern `C { q: x }`, the name `x` is read by these rules, in order: (1) a constructor of the payload's type is a literal pattern; (2) a constructor of another type in scope is `PL1201 TYPE_MISMATCH`, not a binder; (3) any other name binds the payload value. A binder that shadows a constructor in scope is an error (`PL1002`). A binder its arm never reads is `PL1504 UNUSED_BINDER`: write `_` to ignore a payload. An arm that the arms before it always cover is `PL1503 UNREACHABLE_ARM`. So a misspelled constructor cannot become a silent catch-all.
101	  - **Constructors shared by two types (S22).** A constructor name resolves against the expected type. In `a == b` (and `!=`), each side is typed first, and an ambiguous constructor on either side takes the other side's type, so `Open == s` and `s == Open` mean the same. The qualified form `Type.C` is always legal, in expressions and in patterns. A constructor still ambiguous after that is `PL1005 AMBIGUOUS_CONSTRUCTOR`, whose fix is the qualified form.
102	  - **Constructors and blocks.** In the condition of an `if` and the scrutinee of a `match`, an identifier followed by `{` is never read as a constructor: the `{` opens the block. Parentheses restore a constructor there, as in `if intent == (Proposed { decision: 1 }) { … }`. This is the rule Rust uses for the same ambiguity.
